edit(14,[('Stationary windings and a rotating magnetic rotor remove brush contact; cooling can improve, but electronic commutation is still required.','This combines the construction, wear, cooling and switching bullets.'),('Stationary windings remove brush wear and make rotor-position-dependent switching unnecessary.','Removing brushes shifts commutation to electronics rather than removing its function.'),('Rotating armature windings and a segmented commutator give the stated brushless arrangement with external-winding cooling.','This describes the brushed arrangement and mismatches winding location.'),('A rotating magnetic rotor makes bearing friction vanish, so thermal ratings no longer constrain sustained torque.','Brush removal does not remove all mechanical losses or winding heating.')],stem='Which construction-and-control tradeoff matches all four brushless-motor bullets?',why='The source connects stationary external windings to cooling and removal of brush contact to reduced wear, while a position-aware electronic circuit performs commutation.',typ='conceptual')
