edit(20,[('Useful holding requires suitable magnetic response; material, geometry and contact conditions must be checked rather than the metal label alone.','Metallic conductivity does not guarantee useful static magnetic attraction.'),('Equal electrical conductivity is sufficient to give equal magnetic holding for two otherwise similar metal sheets.','Conductivity is not the relevant equality condition for static magnetic response.'),('Equal workpiece mass is sufficient to give equal magnetic holding even if the material response differs.','Mass does not determine magnetic attraction capability.'),('Once surface contact is made, sheet material and air-gap geometry can be omitted from the holding assessment.','Contact alone does not make magnetic response and flux-path geometry irrelevant.')])
edit(21,[('The declared command dimension grows from one to three, but additional independent finger control also changes the feasible task set.','Both command complexity and capability matter.'),('Three independently commanded fingers still give one action coordinate because the object is a single object.','Object count does not collapse independent commands.'),('One opening command gives two independent actions because the simple gripper has two fingers.','Coupled fingers with one command have one declared command degree of freedom.'),('Changing to three commands proves lower sample complexity because it increases manipulation capability.','Greater capability does not alone establish an easier learning problem.')],stem='A two-finger gripper uses one coupled opening command. A proposed three-finger gripper has one independently commanded coordinate per finger. Which conclusion follows for the declared action representations?',why='Action dimension follows independent commands rather than finger count alone; the more expressive gripper may solve tasks the simpler one cannot.',assumptions=['Added command parameterizations; no claim about a particular learning algorithm or sample count.'])
