edit(28,[('Use the MCU for the bounded low-level loop and the SBC for the memory-heavy vision task.','Each workload matches the stated memory and timing conditions.'),('Put the 200 MB vision task on the 256 kB MCU because its control-loop latency is bounded.','Timing suitability does not supply the missing memory.'),('Move the low-level loop solely to the SBC because 4 GB RAM by itself proves a 1 ms scheduling bound.','Memory capacity does not establish scheduler latency.'),('Run both tasks on the MCU because its lower power outweighs the explicit memory requirement.','Lower power does not make the declared 200 MB workload fit.')],stem='In an added allocation exercise, an MCU has 256 kB RAM and a verified 1 ms low-level-loop bound. An SBC has 4 GB RAM and runs a 200 MB vision task, but its worst-case scheduling delay is unspecified. Which allocation is supported?',why='Use the declared timing evidence for low-level control and declared memory requirement for vision; do not infer real-time guarantees from memory or clock rate.',typ='scenario',assumptions=['Added capacities, measured MCU bound and workload; SBC timing remains unspecified.']);premise(28)
edit(32,[('Measure the deployed model’s end-to-end latency at its actual precision, memory traffic and power mode.','Peak throughput alone is not a worst-case latency measurement.'),('Divide model operation count by sparse-INT8 peak TOPS and treat the result as a guaranteed worst-case time for a dense FP32 implementation.','That mixes precision/sparsity conventions and omits noncompute work.'),('Benchmark only one accelerator kernel and use its time as the complete sensing-to-command latency.','Transfers, preprocessing and other stages can contribute.'),('Use a batch-throughput result to infer that every single request meets the same 10 ms latency bound.','Throughput and per-request tail latency are different metrics.')])
edit(33,[('Local is the only demonstrated path meeting 10 ms here; cloud needs a suitable deadline/fallback strategy despite its shorter compute time.','Cloud round-trip plus compute can exceed the deadline.'),('Cloud meets 10 ms because its 2 ms remote computation is faster than the local 8 ms.','This omits the supplied round-trip network delay.'),('Cloud meets 10 ms because the minimum 6 ms round trip plus 2 ms compute fits.','A minimum-delay example is not a worst-case deadline guarantee.'),('Both paths have identical timing because they run the same policy weights.','Different communication paths and timings remain even for the same model.')],stem='In an added 10 ms-deadline comparison, local inference takes a bounded 8 ms. Cloud inference takes 2 ms plus a 6–18 ms network round trip, and outages are possible. Other work is negligible. Which conclusion is supported?',why='The cloud path ranges from 8 to 20 ms before outage handling, while the supplied local bound is 8 ms. More remote compute does not remove communication constraints.',typ='scenario',difficulty='medium',steps=['Local total=8 ms.','Cloud total=2+(6 to 18)=8 to 20 ms; its maximum exceeds 10 ms.'],assumptions=['Added complete path timings for this comparison, not measured 5G performance.'])
