edit(59,[('100 Hz exceeds the supplied 80 Hz ideal bound, but timestamp latency and the complete control design still need checking.','The band-limited signal criterion is separate from the policy frequency and from practical timing guarantees.'),('At least 200 Hz follows from Nyquist because the policy updates at 100 Hz, even though the sensed signal is limited to 40 Hz.','This substitutes policy frequency for the supplied signal bandwidth.'),('A 60 Hz IMU meets the supplied ideal bound because it exceeds the 40 Hz signal bandwidth.','The stated rule requires exceeding twice that bandwidth, not merely exceeding the bandwidth.'),('Meeting the ideal signal-sampling bound proves that the complete estimator and actuator path meet every 10 ms policy deadline.','Signal reconstruction conditions do not establish end-to-end scheduling or communication latency.')],stem='For an added ideal model, the sensed motion is band-limited to 40 Hz and the supplied sampling criterion is f_s > 2B, where B is signal bandwidth. The policy updates at 100 Hz. What can be concluded about a 100 Hz IMU?',typ='scenario',difficulty='medium',why='With B=40 Hz, the supplied ideal requirement is f_s>80 Hz, which 100 Hz satisfies. The policy rate alone does not impose 200 Hz; anti-aliasing, timestamps, estimation and control timing remain separate design considerations.',steps=['The supplied signal model gives 2B=80 Hz.','100 Hz >80 Hz, so this ideal sampling criterion is met.','No bound on complete control latency follows from that inequality.'],forms=[formula('f_s > 2B',{'f_s':'sampling frequency of the declared ideal band-limited sensor signal','B':'highest frequency present in that signal'},{'f_s,B':'Hz'},['Added ideal band-limited reconstruction criterion; not a complete physical IMU/control-system specification.'])],assumptions=['Added strictly band-limited signal model and explicitly supplied ideal sampling criterion; practical filtering and end-to-end delay are not specified.'])
