edit(69,[('Align acquisition times or compensate motion using a justified model; matched arrival times do not prove matched robot poses.','Network/buffer delays can make arrival time differ from measurement time.'),('Pair packets by arrival time because messages reaching the estimator together were necessarily acquired together.','Different transport/buffer delays can violate that assumption.'),('Apply only the fixed sensor-to-body rotation; spatial calibration alone corrects any acquisition-time offset during a turn.','A spatial transform does not by itself align observations of different times.'),('Increase the nominal LiDAR rate while retaining the known clock offset; this alone aligns every scan with the IMU.','Higher rate does not remove a systematic clock offset.')])
edit(71,[('Neither test is justified by six joints alone: A is outside the stated reach bound, and B lacks one independent instantaneous direction.','Workspace reach and local differential capability are separate constraints.'),('A is reachable because six joint coordinates represent six pose coordinates; only B has a limitation.','Counting variables does not defeat the explicitly supplied maximum reach.'),('A is unreachable, but B can realize all six instantaneous directions because all six motors remain enabled.','The supplied rank-five condition means only five independent instantaneous directions.'),('A and B are both unreachable poses because a rank-five Jacobian means the current pose cannot physically exist.','A singular configuration can be physically attained even though local velocity directions are restricted.')],stem='A particular six-joint arm has a maximum base-to-tool distance of 0.8 m. Target A is 1.0 m from the base. At its current attainable pose B, its Jacobian has rank five, meaning five independent instantaneous tool-motion directions. Which conclusion correctly qualifies the source’s flexibility claim?',typ='scenario',difficulty='medium',why='The declared reach bound excludes A. At B, rank five limits local instantaneous motion but does not make the current pose nonexistent. Six joint coordinates alone do not establish arbitrary workspace reach or nonsingularity.',assumptions=['Added arm reach bound and Jacobian-rank interpretation; no particular commercial geometry is assumed.'])
edit(72,[('The changed contact/environment model is a plausible gap even with functioning, calibrated hardware.','The explicit friction mismatch can change dynamics without a hardware fault.'),('The results establish sensor calibration error despite the stipulated independent calibration check.','That attribution ignores the supplied calibration evidence and the stated changed friction.'),('The results establish deadline overruns even though all measured loop timings match the simulation bounds.','The premise removes that particular timing mismatch rather than establishing it.'),('The results establish actuator saturation even though all executed commands were independently verified within the same modelled limits.','The supplied limit check does not support saturation as this case’s explanation.')],stem='In an added sim-to-real diagnosis, sensors are independently calibrated, actuators stay within the modelled limits, and measured timing matches simulation. The simulator uses a high-friction floor, but the real floor is slippery. Which conclusion is supported?',typ='scenario',why='A mismatch in contact/environment modelling can cause different behavior without broken hardware. Hardware nonidealities are one contributor, not an exhaustive definition of the sim-to-real gap.',assumptions=['Added controlled comparison and verified checks; the particular source of the friction change is unspecified.'])
edit(76,[('Use measured end-effector force/torque in an appropriate interaction-control law.','This directly observes the wrench relevant to the stated contact-force response.'),('Use encoder displacement alone and multiply by a presumed contact stiffness that has not been identified.','Unknown contact stiffness prevents that proposed inference from being the most direct measured-wrench route.'),('Use motor current as an exact contact-wrench measurement while neglecting the stipulated unknown transmission friction.','Current-based torque inference can be useful, but the stated unknown friction prevents that exact interpretation.'),('Track the planned position with high gain and infer zero contact force whenever position error is small.','Small position error under contact does not establish small interaction force.')],stem='A manipulator must change motion as contact force rises. Contact stiffness and transmission friction are unknown, but a calibrated end-effector F/T sensor is available. Which feedback configuration most directly supports the source’s compliant-control point?',why='Use the measured interaction wrench with a suitable control law. Position- or current-based inference may require models of the unknown contact and transmission effects, and position tracking alone does not measure contact force.',assumptions=['Added unknown contact stiffness/friction and available calibrated F/T measurement; no safety or closed-loop stability guarantee is claimed.'])

# Final parallelism improvements before freeze.
edit(14,[('Stationary windings and a rotating magnetic rotor remove brush contact; cooling can improve, but electronic commutation is still required.','This combines construction, wear, cooling and switching.'),('Stationary windings and a rotating magnetic rotor improve cooling, but their winding location necessarily lowers sustained torque relative to a similar-size brushed motor.','The source makes the opposite qualified sustained-torque comparison based on better cooling.'),('Rotating armature windings and a segmented commutator give the stated brushless arrangement with external-winding cooling.','This describes the brushed arrangement and mismatches winding location.'),('Stationary windings and a rotating magnetic rotor reduce brush wear, while a mechanical commutator still performs the winding switching.','The source moves switching to an external electronic circuit.')])
edit(18,[('Parallel fingers: inward-sliding flat contacts for box picking; adaptive fingers: wrapping contacts for irregular shapes.','This matches the two source boxes.'),('Parallel fingers: inward-sliding flat contacts that conform around an irregular contour; adaptive fingers: flat contacts constrained to box faces.','This exchanges rigid-flat and wrapping contact roles.'),('Parallel fingers: inward-sliding flat contacts for box picking; adaptive fingers: three rigid contacts that maintain a fixed open aperture throughout the grasp.','The adaptive fingers close and wrap rather than maintaining an unchanged aperture.'),('Parallel fingers: opposing contacts whose shape is adjusted independently at every point on each face; adaptive fingers: wrapping contacts for irregular shapes.','The source parallel mechanism moves flat fingers inward, not independently reshaping every part of each face.')])
